Task 1: Configure Satellite as an Ansible Control Node
Subtask 1.1: Enable Ansible Plugin in Satellite
First, we need to enable the Ansible plugin functionality within Satellite.

Access Satellite Web Interface

# Open your web browser and navigate to your Satellite server
https://satellite.example.com
Login to Satellite

Username: admin
Password: redhat123 (or as provided in your lab environment)
Enable Ansible Plugin

Navigate to Administer → Settings → Ansible

Configure the following settings:

Ansible roles path: /etc/ansible/roles:/usr/share/ansible/roles
Ansible callback: foreman
Ansible inventory: foreman
Install Required Packages on Satellite Server

SSH to your Satellite server and run:

# Install Ansible and required packages
sudo dnf install -y ansible python3-requests

# Install Ansible collections for Satellite
ansible-galaxy collection install theforeman.foreman
Subtask 1.2: Configure Ansible Settings
Create Ansible Configuration File

sudo mkdir -p /etc/ansible
sudo tee /etc/ansible/ansible.cfg > /dev/null << 'EOF'
[defaults]
host_key_checking = False
inventory = /etc/ansible/hosts
roles_path = /etc/ansible/roles:/usr/share/ansible/roles
callback_plugins = /usr/lib/python3.9/site-packages/ansible_runner/display_callback/callback
stdout_callback = foreman

[inventory]
enable_plugins = foreman.foreman

[callback_foreman]
url = https://satellite.example.com
ssl_cert = /etc/pki/katello/certs/katello-apache.crt
ssl_key = /etc/pki/katello/private/katello-apache.key
verify_certs = /etc/pki/katello/certs/katello-default-ca.crt
EOF
Set Proper Permissions

sudo chown -R foreman:foreman /etc/ansible
sudo chmod 644 /etc/ansible/ansible.cfg
Subtask 1.3: Configure Satellite Inventory Plugin
Create Foreman Inventory Configuration

sudo tee /etc/ansible/foreman.yml > /dev/null << 'EOF'
plugin: theforeman.foreman.foreman
url: https://satellite.example.com
user: admin
password: redhat123
validate_certs: False
group_prefix: foreman_
want_facts: True
want_hostcollections: True
want_ansible_ssh_host: True
EOF
Test Inventory Connection

# Test the inventory plugin
ansible-inventory -i /etc/ansible/foreman.yml --list
Task 2: Deploy and Manage Ansible Roles from Satellite
Subtask 2.1: Import Ansible Roles
Access Ansible Roles in Satellite Web UI

Navigate to Configure → Ansible → Roles

Import Roles from Ansible Galaxy

Click Import from [hostname] button to import available roles from the Satellite server.

Install Common Roles via Command Line

# Install useful roles from Ansible Galaxy
sudo ansible-galaxy install geerlingguy.apache
sudo ansible-galaxy install geerlingguy.mysql
sudo ansible-galaxy install linux-system-roles.timesync
sudo ansible-galaxy install linux-system-roles.selinux
Verify Role Installation

# List installed roles
ansible-galaxy list

# Check roles directory
ls -la /etc/ansible/roles/
Subtask 2.2: Create Custom Ansible Role
Create a Custom Role Structure

# Create custom role for system configuration
sudo ansible-galaxy init /etc/ansible/roles/system_config
Define Role Tasks ```bash sudo tee /etc/ansible/roles/system_config/tasks/main.yml > /dev/null << 'EOF'
name: Update system packages yum: name: "*" state: latest when: ansible_os_family == "RedHat"

name: Install essential packages yum: name: - vim - wget - curl - htop state: present

name: Configure timezone timezone: name: "{{ system_timezone | default('America/New_York') }}"

name: Create system user user: name: "{{ system_user | default('sysadmin') }}" groups: wheel append: yes create_home: yes EOF


Define Role Variables ```bash sudo tee /etc/ansible/roles/system_config/defaults/main.yml > /dev/null << 'EOF'
system_timezone: "America/New_York" system_user: "sysadmin" EOF


Subtask 2.3: Import Custom Role into Satellite
Import Roles in Satellite Web UI

Go to Configure → Ansible → Roles and click Import from [satellite-server]

Verify Role Import

You should see your custom roles listed along with the Galaxy roles.

Task 3: Automate Tasks Using Ansible Playbooks from Satellite
Subtask 3.1: Create Ansible Playbooks
Create Playbooks Directory

sudo mkdir -p /etc/ansible/playbooks
sudo chown foreman:foreman /etc/ansible/playbooks
Create System Configuration Playbook ```bash sudo tee /etc/ansible/playbooks/system_setup.yml > /dev/null << 'EOF'
name: System Configuration Playbook hosts: all become: yes vars: system_timezone: "America/New_York" system_user: "sysadmin"

tasks:

name: Include system_config role include_role: name: system_config

name: Configure firewall firewalld: service: ssh permanent: yes state: enabled immediate: yes

name: Start and enable chronyd systemd: name: chronyd state: started enabled: yes

EOF


Create Web Server Deployment Playbook ```bash sudo tee /etc/ansible/playbooks/webserver_deploy.yml > /dev/null << 'EOF'
name: Deploy Apache Web Server hosts: webservers become: yes vars: apache_listen_port: 80 apache_vhosts: - servername: "{{ ansible_fqdn }}" documentroot: "/var/www/html" roles:

geerlingguy.apache
tasks:

name: Create sample index page copy: content: |
Server: {{ ansible_hostname }}
Deployed via Satellite and Ansible

IP Address: {{ ansible_default_ipv4.address }}

dest: /var/www/html/index.html owner: apache group: apache mode: '0644'
EOF ```

Subtask 3.2: Execute Playbooks from Satellite Web Interface
Access Job Templates

Navigate to Hosts → Job Templates

Create New Job Template

Click New Job Template and configure:

Name: System Configuration
Job Category: Ansible Playbook
Provider Type: Ansible
Playbook: system_setup.yml
Inventory: Foreman
Configure Job Template Parameters

# Template inputs (add these in the Inputs tab)
system_timezone:
  input_type: user_input
  description: "System timezone"
  default: "America/New_York"

system_user:
  input_type: user_input
  description: "System user to create"
  default: "sysadmin"
Set Host Selection

In the Locations and Organizations tabs, select appropriate values for your environment.

Subtask 3.3: Run Ansible Jobs
Execute Job Template

Go to Hosts → All Hosts
Select target hosts
Click Select Action → Run Job
Choose your System Configuration template
Click Submit
Monitor Job Execution

Navigate to Monitor → Jobs to view:

Job status and progress
Real-time output
Success/failure status per host
Detailed logs
Run Playbook via Command Line (Alternative)

# Execute playbook directly
ansible-playbook -i /etc/ansible/foreman.yml /etc/ansible/playbooks/system_setup.yml

# Execute with specific host group
ansible-playbook -i /etc/ansible/foreman.yml /etc/ansible/playbooks/webserver_deploy.yml --limit webservers
Subtask 3.4: Create Host Groups with Ansible Roles
Create Host Group with Ansible Roles

Navigate to Configure → Host Groups

Create a new host group:

Name: Web Servers
Ansible Roles: Select geerlingguy.apache and system_config
Assign Hosts to Host Group

Go to Hosts → All Hosts
Edit a host and assign it to the Web Servers host group
Run Ansible Roles on Host Group

Select hosts in the Web Servers group
Click Select Action → Play Ansible roles
Confirm execution
Verification and Testing
Verify Ansible Integration
Check Ansible Configuration

# Verify Ansible can connect to Satellite
ansible --version
ansible-config dump | grep -i foreman
Test Inventory Integration

# List all hosts from Satellite inventory
ansible all -i /etc/ansible/foreman.yml --list-hosts

# Test connectivity
ansible all -i /etc/ansible/foreman.yml -m ping
Verify Role Execution

# Check if services are running on managed hosts
ansible all -i /etc/ansible/foreman.yml -m service -a "name=chronyd state=started" --become
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Inventory Plugin Not Working

# Check Python dependencies
sudo dnf install -y python3-requests python3-pyyaml

# Verify Satellite API access
curl -k -u admin:redhat123 https://satellite.example.com/api/v2/hosts
Issue 2: Role Import Failures

# Check role permissions
sudo chown -R foreman:foreman /etc/ansible/roles
sudo chmod -R 755 /etc/ansible/roles

# Restart Satellite services
sudo foreman-maintain service restart
Issue 3: Job Execution Failures

# Check Ansible logs
sudo tail -f /var/log/foreman/production.log

# Verify SSH connectivity
ansible all -i /etc/ansible/foreman.yml -m setup --limit hostname
Conclusion
In this lab, you have successfully:

Configured Satellite as an Ansible Control Node: You integrated Ansible functionality directly into Red Hat Satellite, enabling centralized automation management.

Deployed and Managed Ansible Roles: You imported roles from Ansible Galaxy, created custom roles, and managed them through the Satellite interface.

Automated Tasks Using Playbooks: You created and executed Ansible playbooks from Satellite, demonstrating how to automate system configuration and application deployment.

Why This Matters: The integration of Ansible with Satellite provides a powerful combination for enterprise system management. This integration allows you to:

Leverage Satellite's inventory management with Ansible's automation capabilities
Centralize configuration management and compliance reporting
Scale automation across large infrastructures efficiently
Maintain audit trails and job history through Satellite's interface
Combine patch management with configuration automation
This knowledge is essential for the Red Hat Certified Specialist in Deployment and Systems Management exam and provides practical skills for managing enterprise Linux environments at scale. The ability to integrate these tools effectively makes you valuable in organizations seeking to modernize their infrastructure management practices.
